ACLERICALLGOLD      T0He looks at you kindly. 'I now really know how badly you want to
ACLERICALLGOLD      T1contribute.  You don't quite have that much, but I will take what you've
ACLERICALLGOLD      T1got.  Bless you, my friend.  You have restored my faith in adventurers.'
ACLERICBADDONATE    T0Although he is the silent type, you can tell you have offended him by your
ACLERICBADDONATE    T1affrontery. He waves his hands and dissappears. All of a sudden, you don't
ACLERICBADDONATE    T1feel so good.
ACLERICCURESICKNESS T0After several minutes of prayer, he passes out from strain. Although
ACLERICCURESICKNESS T1he looks a little worse for wear, you certainly feel better.
ACLERICLEREND       H0                                 THE CLERIC
ACLERICLEREND       T2You enter a dimly lit room containing many books, potions, and magical
ACLERICLEREND       T1artifacts. Behind the counter, a wizened old man seems to appear from
ACLERICLEREND       T1nowhere. 'Good day, traveler' he says warmly.  'My name is Lerend.  I
ACLERICLEREND       T1am the resident cleric What can I do for you?'
ACLERICNOGOLD       T0As you go for your money, you realize just how poor you are.  Begging his
ACLERICNOGOLD       T1forgiveness, you walk away.
ACLERICNOREMOVE     T0After examining your donation, he states that the stars are out of
ACLERICNOREMOVE     T1cosmic order, the portents tell of dread, and that the next time you fight a
ACLERICNOREMOVE     T1monster, someone mightly will die.
ACLERICNOREMOVE     T2You become so wrapped up in his prediction that you fail to notice that
ACLERICNOREMOVE     T1nothing was removed.
ACLERICREMOVE       T0You ask the cleric to rid you of the item
ACLERICREMOVE       T2With almost superhuman strength, undoubtedly enhanced supernaturally, he
ACLERICREMOVE       T1rips the item from your possession.  noting the tag on the bottom of the item
ACLERICREMOVE       T1which says 'DISPOSE OF PROPERLY', he throws the item in the trash.
ACLERICSTAYSICK     T0He diagnoses your sickness, then he diagnoses your donation.  With a
ACLERICSTAYSICK     T1disappointing look and reproachfullness in his voice, he sadly states
ACLERICSTAYSICK     T1that he can't help you.
ACLERICWANDERER     H0                                 THE CLERIC
ACLERICWANDERER     T2The man who has just approached you is dressed in some type of hooded
ACLERICWANDERER     T1robe.  You perceive the faint smell of incense as he begins to speak.
ACLERICWANDERER     T1'Good day, traveler' he says warmly.  'I am a cleric who is intent
ACLERICWANDERER     T1on furthering the good in this place.  What can I do for you?'
ASHOPKIERA          H0                                  THE SHOP
ASHOPKIERA          T2You enter a bright but musty room containing every imaginable weapon, armor,
ASHOPKIERA          T1or cloak.  Behind the counter, a sultry yet cheerful young woman greets
ASHOPKIERA          T1you.  'Good day, traveler', she says warmly.  'My name is Kiera.  What can I
ASHOPKIERA          T1do for you today?'
ASHOPNOBUY          T0She looks a little dispondent but quickly cheers up. 'You can't blame a
ASHOPNOBUY          T1girl for trying' she says jovially.  The way she was eyeing your moneysack,
ASHOPNOBUY          T1you begin to think that you'll be lucky to have any gold left at the end of
ASHOPNOBUY          T1this shopping excursion.
ASHOPNOGOLD         T0As you rummage through your moneysack, you sadly realize that you are a
ASHOPNOGOLD         T1little short on funds.  You give her the puppy dog look that got you
ASHOPNOGOLD         T1a free beer at the local tavern. 'Hey! I've tried that look before' she
ASHOPNOGOLD         T1chuckles. 'It really works at the local tavern (but evidently not here).
ASHOPNOGOLD         T1Come back when you have a little more gold, and maybe we can do business!'
ASHOPRECHARGE       T0She takes the wand, mumbles a few words that you can't quite hear, and
ASHOPRECHARGE       T1brings the item back to you.  After assuring you that the item has been
ASHOPRECHARGE       T1recharged, she hands the item back to you.  You can't help but wonder if
ASHOPRECHARGE       T1it was worth the trouble, but her disarming smile and light conversation
ASHOPRECHARGE       T1soon have you thinking of other treasures and adventures.
ASHOPTRAVELER       H0                                  THE SHOP
ASHOPTRAVELER       T2The traveler opens her coat.  It contains every imaginable weapon, armor,
ASHOPTRAVELER       T1or cloak.  The salesperson eyes you with an appraising eye.
ASHOPTRAVELER       T1'Good day, traveler', she says warmly.  'I am a traveling salesperson.
ASHOPTRAVELER       T1What can I sell you today?'
CARD0               T1Now you are totally healed!
CARD1               T1All of your posessions are stipped from you!
CARD2               T1You are granted three wishes!
CARD3               T1All statistics now are raised two!
CARD4               T1All statistics now are lowered two!
CARD5               T1Your constitution is doubled (maximum 21)!
CARD6               T1Your constitution is halved (minimum 1)!
CARD7               T1Your hitpoints are enhanced!
CARD8               T1You loose hitpoints!
CREATEUSER          H              SORCERER'S BANE (v1.1) -- NEW PLAYER INFORMATION
CREATEUSER          T2Welcome to Sorcerer's Bane (or SB, for short).  SB has been set up at this
CREATEUSER          T1installation to be multi-user.  This enforces password protection to protect
CREATEUSER          T1everyone's playing privileges.  Each player has a player name AND password
CREATEUSER          T1that YOU HAVE TO REMEMBER when playing this game.
CREATEUSER          T2Some player names are invalid, especially if they use too many special
CREATEUSER          T1characters (i.e. !@#$%^&*()_-=+.,<>/?, etc.).  This is because the player
CREATEUSER          T1names are used as part of the multi-user locking system exclusively used by SB.
CREATEUSER          T2If the name you enter is in use, you will be asked the password.  If you fail
CREATEUSER          T1to give the proper password, you will be returned to the system.  If the name
CREATEUSER          T1you enter is not in use, you will be asked if you wish to create it.
CREATEUSER          T2Please REMEMBER YOUR PLAYER NAME AND PASSWORD!! (Write them down if necessary.)
CREATEUSER          T1Also, don't create multiple players. Each player can create many characters.
CREATEUSER          T1Don't make life hard for the SB administrator, for your sake as well as his.
DECK                T This gambler seems to be more civilized than most gamblers you've seen.
DECK                B2'Hello, my friend.  I am a stranger to these parts.'
DECK                T0, he says.  You believe him
DECK                T1since he's far too civilized to NOT be a stranger to these parts.
DECK                B2Like you, I sought battle with the evil sorcerer at the bottom of this dungeon.
DECK                B1I barely escaped with my life, but not before I was cursed to carry the DECK OF
DECK                B1MANY THINGS around with me.  I am compelled to offer any adventurer who does
DECK                B1not threaten me as many draws from the deck as he or she would like.'
DECK                T2He then hesitates as if trying to fight the geas.  Then he continues.
DECK                B2'Be warned, my friend.  I have seen adventurers rise to great heights by drawing
DECK                B1from this deck, yet I have also seen their very existance snuffed out.  I
DECK                B1would advise not drawing, for there are no easy roads to greatness.  However,
DECK                B1the choice is yours.'
DECK                H2DO YOU WANT TO DRAW A CARD FROM THE DECK OF MANY THINGS?
DOSHAREWARE         H                  Copyright 1992 Wood Software Development
DOSHAREWARE         T2Hi! This is Chuck Wood, the author of Sorcerer's Bane.  I hope you have
DOSHAREWARE         T1enjoyed playing my game.This game is not free, but is distributed as
DOSHAREWARE         T1shareware. That means that if you like Sorcerer's Bane, you would pay the 
DOSHAREWARE         T1reasonable registration fee.  It also means that you are not only allowed but
DOSHAREWARE         T1but encouraged to make FULL (please!) copies of this product and pass them on
DOSHAREWARE         T1to others.
DOSHAREWARE         T2Registration will be its own reward.  For only 19.95, you will not only 
DOSHAREWARE         T1receive the most up-to-date version of my game, but will also get my "Game
DOSHAREWARE         T1Master" (GM) set of programs. These will allow you to add items and monsters,
DOSHAREWARE         T1edit character stats, and resurrect characters from the Hall of Fame list.
DOSHAREWARE         T2For only $99.95, you receive all of the above PLUS a copy of SB and GM source
DOSHAREWARE         T1code, a royalty-free version of BASWOOD (with source) which is a BASIC 
DOSHAREWARE         T1development library that includes keyboard control, mouse control, color 
DOSHAREWARE         T1control, screen and field entry, and an excellent pull-down menu handler 
DOSHAREWARE         T1(that is used throughout SB).  Printed documentation for all the software is
DOSHAREWARE         T1also included.  All code is completely self-contained and written ENTIRELY in
DOSHAREWARE         T1MICROSOFT(R) QUICKBASIC(TM), so it should be easy to customize.
DOSHAREWARE         H                  Copyright 1992 Wood Software Development
DOSHAREWARE         T2Your registration fee entitles you to use all software received on a single
DOSHAREWARE         T1computer, make as many backups as you wish, and/or transfer all rights to
DOSHAREWARE         T1the software to another individual.  Although you cannot use the source code
DOSHAREWARE         T1for SB or GM for distribution without written expressed permission of the 
DOSHAREWARE         T1holder of the copyright, you can use the BASWOOD utilities to make and
DOSHAREWARE         T1distribute your own software ALONG WITH the BASWOOD.LIB that will come with
DOSHAREWARE         T1the 99.95 registration.  Site licenses are also available; call for details.
DOSHAREWARE         H2WHAT A DEAL!!!
DOSHAREWARE         T2So don't delay!  Print the registration form, fill it out, and send it in with
DOSHAREWARE         T1your check or money order today! (No cash please, for my sake as well as yours,
DOSHAREWARE         T1and all checks must be drawn against U.S. banks.)  Make all checks out for 
DOSHAREWARE         T1either $19.95 (for the SB regular registration) or 99.95 (for the SB extended
DOSHAREWARE         T1registration). And by all means, enjoy the game!  Don't forget to eat, sleep,
DOSHAREWARE         T1and go to work while you are playing.
DRAGONSLAIR         H                                  DRAGON'S LAIR
DRAGONSLAIR         T2You have entered a dragon's lair!  Contrary to popular opinion, you don't find
DRAGONSLAIR         T1any treasure chests or precious items lying around.  However, the whole area
DRAGONSLAIR         T1smells like smoke, and is at least 20 degrees hotter than on the outside.
DRAGONSLAIR         T1The owner of the lair then appears and starts advancing on you!  It's bigger
DRAGONSLAIR         T1than your last date! (Boy, was that a huge one.)  It towers over you and begins
DRAGONSLAIR         T1to speak.
DRAGONSLAIR         R2'I am a guardian of the staff.  Procede at your own peril!'
DRAGONSLAIR         T2Hmmm. This may be just a teensy-weensy bit difficult.
GAMBLER             T
GAMBLER             T1The gambler reaches in his cloak and pulls out a deck of cards!
GAMBLER             B2'Hey buddy!'
GAMBLER             T0, the gambler intones,
GAMBLER             B0 'I'm feelin' pretty lucky today.  Tell
GAMBLER             B1ya what I'm gonna do for ya.  I have this deck, with cards numbered duece
GAMBLER             B1through ace. What say you and me cut the deck, and wager on what's outcomin'?
GAMBLER             H9DO YOU WANT TO WAGER WITH THE GAMBLER?
GAMBLERNOBET        T2Disgruntled with your obvious lack of ability, the gambler leaves.
GAMBLERNOGOLD       B2'Sorry chum'
GAMBLERNOGOLD       T0, the gambler scoffs,
GAMBLERNOGOLD       B0 'I don't lend to deadbeats like yourself. You
GAMBLERNOGOLD       B1don't have that much money, and bein' the shady character that you are, I
GAMBLERNOGOLD       B1don't place much value on your marker.  After all, bein' a computer-generated
GAMBLERNOGOLD       B1construct, my legal rights to youse users is suspect, y'know?  It ain't too
GAMBLERNOGOLD       B1easy to hire two goons to break your legs neither.'
GAMBLERLOOSES       B1'Hmph! I loose.'
GAMBLERLOOSES       T0, the gambler laments as you smile broadly.
GAMBLERTIES         T1It was a tie!
GAMBLERTIES         B0 'Nobody wins'
GAMBLERTIES         T0, the gambler grumbles
GAMBLERWINS         B1'I win!  BWAA-HA-HA-HA-HA!'
GAMBLERWINS         T0, the gambler gloats.
GAMBLERTRY          T1Would you like to try again?
GETUSER             H                    PLAYER SELECTION
GETUSER             T2If you have played SB since this systems installation, personal information
GETUSER             T1(containing system defaults, existing players, etc.) is already on file.  If
GETUSER             T1you have not, you will need to create a new player (containing system defaults,
GETUSER             T1existing players, etc.)  If you already have stored your player information,
GETUSER             T1please DO NOT create a new player.
GOLDDRAGON          T1'Hmm,' the dragon ponders.  'You certainly seem brave enough.  I suppose
GOLDDRAGON          T1anyone who tries to talk to a dragon is brave, in a stupid kind of way.
GOLDDRAGON          T1Very well, I will give you the Gold Quarterstff.'  You quickly take the
GOLDDRAGON          T1quarterstaff and leave all your gold in return. Miraculously, you escape
GOLDDRAGON          T1with your life.
GOLDDRAGON          T2You hear the dragon as you leave.  'Thanks for the gold.  It's my favorite
GOLDDRAGON          T1color.  Better get some more soon, though, or who will pay Lerend to heal
GOLDDRAGON          T1you after you've been killed by the wizard.  BWAA-HA-HA-HA-HA.'
GOLDDRAGON          T2Some monsters sure have a warped sense of humor!
HELPCHAR            H               CHARACTER MAITENANCE MENU HELP FOR SORCERER'S BANE
HELPCHAR            H2(C) CREATE A NEW CHARACTER
HELPCHAR            T1This option lets you design a character suitable for running in
HELPCHAR            T1Sorcerer's Bane.  You will be able to choose a character's main statistics,
HELPCHAR            T1race, and class.  You must create a character before using one.
HELPCHAR            H2(U) USE AN EXISTING CHARACTER
HELPCHAR            T1This option lets you use a previously created character in Sorcerer's Bane.
HELPCHAR            T1The character you pick can visit the cleric or the shop or run in the dungeon
HELPCHAR            H2(C) LIST ALL CHARACTERS
HELPCHAR            T1This option lists all the characters currently running in Sorcerer's Bane.
HELPCHAR            H2(A) SHOW A CHARACTER'S ATTRIBUTES
HELPCHAR            T1This option shows the attributes of an existing character.
HELPCHAR            H2(F) FIND THE EXPERIENCE FOR A LEVEL
HELPCHAR            T1This option allows you to find the experience level needed to reach different
HELPCHAR            T1levels for different classes.
HELPCHAR            H               CHARACTER MAITENANCE MENU HELP FOR SORCERER'S BANE
HELPCHAR            H2(K) KILL AN EXISTING CHARACTER
HELPCHAR            T1This option 'kills' an existing character and releases any disk space this
HELPCHAR            T1character may be using.
HELPCHAR            H2(D) CHANGE SYSTEM DEFAULTS
HELPCHAR            T1This option allows you to change certain defaults in Sorcerer's Bane like sound
HELPCHAR            T1and graphics.
HELPCHAR            H2(H) HELP
HELPCHAR            T1Help prints this summary
HELPCHAR            H2(I) INTRODUCTION
HELPCHAR            T1This option reprints the introduction.  A MUST for new users.  This will answer
HELPCHAR            T1many questions you have about the game.
HELPCHAR            H2(P) PRINT THE REGISTRATION FORM
HELPCHAR            T1This option prints the registration form so you can become a registered user.
HELPCHAR            H2(Q or ESC) QUIT THE GAME
HELPCHAR            T1As stated, this option quits the game and returns you to DOS
HELPCLASS           H                      CLASS DESCRIPTION IN SORCERER'S BANE
HELPCLASS           T1Your class tells what kind of character you are and what powers you have.  The
HELPCLASS           T1brief description following will describe each class:
HELPCLASS           H2FIGHTER
HELPCLASS           T1A fighter is good at fighting.  He does not cast spells or sing songs
HELPCLASS           T1like a mage or a bard, but he can use magical items.  A fighter should
HELPCLASS           T1have good strength and dexterity.  Humans and Trolls make good
HELPCLASS           T1fighters.
HELPCLASS           H2MAGE
HELPCLASS           T1A mage, or magician, is the spell-caster.  He can fight, especially
HELPCLASS           T1when he runs out of spells, but spells are what he usually uses
HELPCLASS           T1if possible.  A mage should have high intelligence.  Elves, Dwarves,
HELPCLASS           T1and Gnomes make good mages.
HELPCLASS           H2BARD
HELPCLASS           T1A bard fights well, but not as good as a fighter.  He sings songs
HELPCLASS           T1that aid in fighting or healing.  Songs are different in nature from
HELPCLASS           T1spells.  Songs usually work slower but are more constant than spells.
HELPCLASS           T1A bard also never runs out of songs once he learns them.  However, his
HELPCLASS           T1songs are every bit as powerful as spells, but are fewer in number.
HELPCLASS           T1Any class can make a good bard.
HELPCLERIC          H                      OPTIONS USED IN THE CLERIC'S TEMPLE
HELPCLERIC          H3(C) CURE WOUNDS
HELPCLERIC          T1This option immediately restores hit points lost in the dungeon.  Hitpoints
HELPCLERIC          T1also regenerate over time.
HELPCLERIC          H2(S) HEAL A SICKNESS
HELPCLERIC          T1This option removes a sickness.  Since sickness makes it difficult to 
HELPCLERIC          T1automatically regenerate hit points, you probably want rid of it.  Sickness
HELPCLERIC          T1can also be removed by certain magical items, by a wish, or sometimes at the
HELPCLERIC          T1fountains found in the dungeon.
HELPCLERIC          H2(R) REMOVE A CURSED ITEM
HELPCLERIC          T1If you pick up a cursed item and WEAR or WIELD it before knowing what it is,
HELPCLERIC          T1you will probably want it removed.  Lerend can do that
HELPCLERIC          H                      OPTIONS USED IN THE CLERIC'S TEMPLE
HELPCLERIC          H3(I) INVENTORY MANAGEMENT
HELPCLERIC          T1This option allows the character to wear, wield, or use any item in his 
HELPCLERIC          T1inventory, and to perform other inventory-related functions.
HELPCLERIC          H2(H) HELP 
HELPCLERIC          T1Help prints this message
HELPCLERIC          H2(Q OR ESC) RETURN TO MAIN MENU
HELPCLERIC          T1When you are finished at the cleric's temple, hit 'Q' or 'ESC to return to the
HELPCLERIC          T1character menu.
HELPDEFAULTS        H                   DEFAULT OPTIONS IN SORCERER'S BANE 
HELPDEFAULTS        T2The following options let you choose the system defaults used in Sorcerer's
HELPDEFAULTS        T1Bane:
HELPDEFAULTS        H2(C) TURN THE COLOR ON/OFF
HELPDEFAULTS        T1This allows you to toggle your game to color or monochrome.  Laptop LCD users
HELPDEFAULTS        T1will probably want the color turned off.
HELPDEFAULTS        H2(S) TURN THE SOUND (MUSIC) ON/OFF
HELPDEFAULTS        T1Hey, I know that it's a great theme song and you could listen to it for hours,
HELPDEFAULTS        T1(I like to call it "Bastardized Beethoven"), but should you be so inclined,
HELPDEFAULTS        T1you could toggle the music off and on with this option.
HELPDEFAULTS        H2(E) TURN THE ERROR ALARM ON/OFF
HELPDEFAULTS        T1Those beeps can get annoying, especailly late at night or on the job.  This
HELPDEFAULTS        T1option turns off the error beep.
HELPDEFAULTS        H                   DEFAULT OPTIONS IN SORCERER'S BANE 
HELPDEFAULTS        T2The following options let you choose the system defaults used in Sorcerer's
HELPDEFAULTS        T1Bane:
HELPDEFAULTS        H2(M) TURN THE MOUSE ON/OFF
HELPDEFAULTS        T1This game works fine with a numeric keypad, or even a 4-key arrow keypad.  It
HELPDEFAULTS        T1also works with a mouse.  You can turn this option off, especially if you are
HELPDEFAULTS        T1running this in OS/2 2.0 partial screen. (This hurts my mouse control.)  This
HELPDEFAULTS        T1option will not display if you do not have a recognizable mouse.
HELPDEFAULTS        H2(D) TURN THE MOUSE BUTTON DESCRIPTION ON/OFF
HELPDEFAULTS        T1In SB, the two mouse buttons usually stay pretty consistant.  The left button
HELPDEFAULTS        T1means ENTER (choose, etc.).  The right button means ESC (Quit, Run, No, etc.).
HELPDEFAULTS        T1The actual meaning can be displayed if this option is on.
HELPDEFAULTS        H2(H) HELP
HELPDEFAULTS        T1Help displays this text.
HELPDEFAULTS        H2(Q OR ESC) SAVE & GO TO MAIN OR PREVIOUS MENU
HELPDEFAULTS        T1This option always saves the default information.  It then usually goes back
HELPDEFAULTS        T1to the previous calling menu UNLESS it is on a new player or when Single user
HELPDEFAULTS        T1is defined.  It will then go to the MAIN MENU.
HELPFIGHT           H               MONSTER INTERACTION OPTIONS IN SORCERER'S BANE 
HELPFIGHT           H2(F) FIGHT
HELPFIGHT           T1This option tells the character to do one round of fighting with this monster
HELPFIGHT           H2(K) KILL 
HELPFIGHT           T1This option tells the character to continue fight to the death with the 
HELPFIGHT           T1monster.  It is typically used for fighting easy-to-kill creatures. 
HELPFIGHT           H2(T) TALK 
HELPFIGHT           T1This option tells the character to try and either reason with this creature
HELPFIGHT           T1or at least soothe it with gentle talking (i.e. "Nice Doggie").  This will 
HELPFIGHT           T1always work with good creatures, sometimes work with neutral creatures, and
HELPFIGHT           T1never work with inherently evil creatures.
HELPFIGHT           H2(R), (ESC) RUN
HELPFIGHT           T1This option tells the character to try to get away.
HELPFIGHT           H2(M) MONSTER INFO
HELPFIGHT           T1This option tells you about the monster you are facing.  It also compares the
HELPFIGHT           T1monster's extended attributes with your own.
HELPFIGHT           H               MONSTER INTERACTION OPTIONS IN SORCERER'S BANE 
HELPFIGHT           H2(C) CAST 
HELPFIGHT           T1This option tells a mage to cast a spell.  This will only work with mages.
HELPFIGHT           H2(S) SING A SONG
HELPFIGHT           T1This option tells a bard to sing a magical song.  This will only work with 
HELPFIGHT           T1bards.
HELPFIGHT           H2(I) INVENTORY MANAGEMENT
HELPFIGHT           T1This option allows the character to use his inventory.  This always takes one
HELPFIGHT           T1round, but allows the character to exchange multiple items, or use 1 item.
HELPFIGHT           H2(W) WISH
HELPFIGHT           T1This option allows the character to make a wish.  Wishes have to be granted
HELPFIGHT           T1or found, and this always takes at least one round.
HELPFIGHT           H2(H) HELP
HELPFIGHT           T1Help displays this text.
HELPMAIN            H              CHARACTER MENU HELP FOR SORCERER'S BANE
HELPMAIN            H2(R) RUN AN EXISTING CHARACTER
HELPMAIN            T1This option lets you run a previously created character in the dungeon.  Only
HELPMAIN            T1by running a character initially can you gain gold and experience.
HELPMAIN            H2(A) SHOW A CHARACTER'S ATTRIBUTES
HELPMAIN            T1This option shows the attributes of an existing character.
HELPMAIN            H2(S) HAVE A CHARACTER VISIT THE SHOP
HELPMAIN            T1This option lets the character visit the shop.  Although you can visit the shop
HELPMAIN            T1with no money, it won't help you.  To get gold, run in the dungeon.
HELPMAIN            H2(C) HAVE A CHARACTER VISIT THE CLERIC
HELPMAIN            T1This option lets the character visit the cleric.  The cleric can heal you and
HELPMAIN            T1remove cursed items and sickness that you may have acquired in the dungeon.
HELPMAIN            T1To get gold, run in the dungeon.
HELPMAIN            H2(W) MAKE A WISH
HELPMAIN            T1This option allows a character to make a wish.  A character must be granted
HELPMAIN            T1a wish either in the dungeon or by a magical item before he can make a wish.
HELPMAIN            T1levels for different classes
HELPMAIN            H              CHARACTER MENU HELP FOR SORCERER'S BANE
HELPMAIN            H2(F) FIND THE EXPERIENCE FOR A LEVEL
HELPMAIN            T1This option allows you to find the experience level needed to reach different
HELPMAIN            T1levels for different classes.
HELPMAIN            H2(I) INTRODUCTION
HELPMAIN            T1This option reprints the introduction.  A MUST for new users.  This will answer
HELPMAIN            T1many questions you have about the game.
HELPMAIN            H2(D) CHANGE SYSTEM DEFAULTS
HELPMAIN            T1This option allows you to change certain defaults in Sorcerer's Bane like sound
HELPMAIN            T1and color.
HELPMAIN            H2(P) PRINT THE REGISTRATION FORM
HELPMAIN            T1This option prints the registration form so you can become a registered user.
HELPMAIN            H2(H) HELP
HELPMAIN            T1Help prints this summary
HELPMAIN            H2(Q or ESC) QUIT THE GAME
HELPMAIN            T1As stated, this option quits the game and returns you to DOS
HELPRACE            H                       RACE DESCRIPTION IN SORCERER'S BANE
HELPRACE            T2Your race should be chosen to reflect what kind of character you wish to be.
HELPRACE            T1All races except Human affect your statistics, either raising them or lowering
HELPRACE            T1them as that race relates to the Human race in Sorcerer's Bane.  Please note
HELPRACE            T1that some of the description might not match up to other adventure games, but
HELPRACE            T1for various reasons, these are the one's used in Sorcerer's Bane.
HELPRACE            H2HUMAN
HELPRACE            T1A human has the traits that all other races are measured by in
HELPRACE            T1Sorcerer's Bane.  As the second strongest race, Humans make good
HELPRACE            T1fighters, but can excel in any class.
HELPRACE            H2TROLL
HELPRACE            T1A Troll is a creature from the bitter Coldlands. Surviving in such
HELPRACE            T1a harsh environment has made Trolls a very hardy and strong race,
HELPRACE            T1while at the same time not doing much for their appearance.  Trolls
HELPRACE            T1rely on strength, hardiness, and agility to survive.  They are not
HELPRACE            T1great thinkers.  Like the elves, Trolls scorn luck as unreliable,
HELPRACE            T1preferring to take matters into their own hands.
HELPRACE            H                       RACE DESCRIPTION IN SORCERER'S BANE
HELPRACE            T2Your race should be chosen to reflect what kind of character you wish to be.
HELPRACE            T1All races except Human affect your statistics, either raising them or lowering
HELPRACE            T1them as that race relates to the Human race in Sorcerer's Bane.  Please note
HELPRACE            T1that some of the description might not match up to other adventure games, but
HELPRACE            T1for various reasons, these are the one's used in Sorcerer's Bane.
HELPRACE            H2ELF
HELPRACE            T1An Elf is a creature from the woodlands.  They are considered beautiful
HELPRACE            T1and magical.  Though not as hardy or strong as a Human, they usually
HELPRACE            T1are more intelligent and more nimble.  Elves tend to scorn luck as
HELPRACE            T1unreliable.  Elves make good magicians.
HELPRACE            H2DWARF
HELPRACE            T1Dwarves usually live underground.  They are famous for their work with
HELPRACE            T1metal and mining.  They tend to be somewhat smaller than Humans.  This
HELPRACE            T1allows them to fit into smaller places, like the mines.  They are not as
HELPRACE            T1strong as Humans due to their size, but they make up for it with their
HELPRACE            T1hardiness and agility.  Dwarves tend to be more intelligent than Humans
HELPRACE            T1due to much self-reflecting. Like the Elves and the Trolls, Dwarves
HELPRACE            T1scorn luck as a weakling's way out. Dwarves can excell in any class.
HELPRACE            H                       RACE DESCRIPTION IN SORCERER'S BANE
HELPRACE            T2Your race should be chosen to reflect what kind of character you wish to be.
HELPRACE            T1All races except Human affect your statistics, either raising them or lowering
HELPRACE            T1them as that race relates to the Human race in Sorcerer's Bane.  Please note
HELPRACE            T1that some of the description might not match up to other adventure games, but
HELPRACE            T1for various reasons, these are the one's used in Sorcerer's Bane.
HELPRACE            H2GNOME
HELPRACE            T1Gnomes share the woodland with the Elves.  They are not as beautiful as
HELPRACE            T1the Humans and the Elves, due to their short and pudgy stature.  Gnomes
HELPRACE            T1live in trees and are very close to nature.  They are the weakest of all
HELPRACE            T1the classes.  They tend to live in milder climates, and are not too
HELPRACE            T1hardy. Unlike the Elves, Trolls, and Drarves, Gnomes view luck as
HELPRACE            T1desirable and someone who is lucky as close to nature.  They are nimble
HELPRACE            T1and have an enlightened outlook on life.  They make very good magicians.
HELPRUN             H            COMMANDS FOR SORCERER'S BANE WHILE RUNNING A CHARACTER
HELPRUN             H2MOVEMENT OPTIONS
HELPRUN             T1(), (8)    - up (north)                   
HELPRUN             T1(), (2)    - down (south)     
HELPRUN             T1(->) (4)    - left (west)                  
HELPRUN             T1() (6)     - right (east)     
HELPRUN             T1(HOME), (7) - up-left (north-west)
HELPRUN             T1(END), (1)  - down-left (south-west)
HELPRUN             T1(PGUP), (9) - up-right (north-east)
HELPRUN             T1(PGDN), (3) - down-right (south-east)                
HELPRUN             H2INFORMATION
HELPRUN             T1(X) - Display a character's extended attributes
HELPRUN             T1(I) - Inventory management (Wear, wield, or use an item)
HELPRUN             T1(H) - Help (This screen)
HELPRUN             H2MISCELLANEOUS
HELPRUN             T1(W)          - Make a wish (Wishes have to be found or granted.)
HELPRUN             T1(C)          - Cast a magical spell (mages only)
HELPRUN             T1(S)          - Sing a magical song (bards only)
HELPRUN             T1(INS), (0)   - Toggle running mode on/off   
HELPRUN             T1(Q), (ESC)   - Quit running and return to CHARACTER MAIN MENU
HELPRUN             H                      MAP KEY/CHARACTER REPRESENTATION
HELPRUN             T4                      - Upstairs               - Downstairs
HELPRUN             T3   ë                   - Fountain          —     - Throne
HELPRUN             T3                      - Trap              $     - Treasure
HELPRUN             T3   ≥ ƒ ¡ ¬ ⁄ ø Ÿ ¿     - Walls             ∫ Õ   - Doors
HELPRUN             T3   D                   - Dragon            W     - Wizard
HELPRUN             T3   ù                   - Player
HELPSHOP            H                           OPTIONS USED IN THE SHOP
HELPSHOP            H3(B) BUY AN ITEM
HELPSHOP            T1This option allows you to buy an item in the shop.  Only non-magical items
HELPSHOP            T1can be purchased here, although the shop keeper can identify any item.
HELPSHOP            H2(S) SELL AN ITEM
HELPSHOP            T1This option allows a character to sell an item that is no longer needed or
HELPSHOP            T1used.  With the exception of a BAZERKER SWORD, only non-cursed items can be
HELPSHOP            T1sold.  Also, you cannot sell an item you are wearing or wielding.
HELPSHOP            H2(K) IDENTIFY AN ITEM
HELPSHOP            T1Identifying an item allows the shopkeeper to tell you what an item is, whether
HELPSHOP            T1it is cursed or not, and it gives you the ability to sell the item.
HELPSHOP            H2(R) RECHARGE A WAND
HELPSHOP            T1Wand can expend charges over time.  The shopkeeper can recharge a wand.  This
HELPSHOP            T1will never destroy the wand, but it can actually lessen the charges in a wand,
HELPSHOP            T1especially a fully-charged one.  As this is the secret art of the shopkeeper
HELPSHOP            T1and identified items don't tell the number of charges, you never know if it
HELPSHOP            T1worked or not.  Still, it is a good idea to do on a wand that has been used 
HELPSHOP            T1with regularity.
HELPSHOP            H                           OPTIONS USED IN THE SHOP
HELPSHOP            H3(I) INVENTORY MANAGEMENT
HELPSHOP            T1This option allows the character to wear, wield, or use any item in his 
HELPSHOP            T1inventory, and to perform other inventory-related functions.
HELPSHOP            H2(H) HELP 
HELPSHOP            T1Help prints this message
HELPSHOP            H2(Q OR ESC) RETURN TO MAIN MENU
HELPSHOP            T1When you are finished at the shop, hit 'Q' or 'ESC to return to the
HELPSHOP            T1character menu.
HELPSTATS           H                   STATISTIC DESCRIPTION IN SORCERER'S BANE
HELPSTATS           T2The six main statistics, along with random events, determine how well your
HELPSTATS           T1character will perform in Sorcerer's Bane.  The following is a description
HELPSTATS           T1of each one and how each one affects you.  Unlike typical adventure games,
HELPSTATS           T1each statistic will affect the majority of events in Sorcerer's Bane.  (i.e.
HELPSTATS           T1a smart fighter will fight better than a stupid one.)
HELPSTATS           H2STR
HELPSTATS           T1Strength - Strength describes how physically strong your character is. It
HELPSTATS           T1           primarily determines how much damage you do once you hit an
HELPSTATS           T1           opponent, but also affects your hitting ability, your protection
HELPSTATS           T1           protection and your ability to talk to and purchase from the shop.
HELPSTATS           H2INT
HELPSTATS           T1Intelligence - Intelligence mainly affects your ability to use magic,
HELPSTATS           T1               magical items, and resist magic, and your alertness.  It
HELPSTATS           T1               also affects your hitting ability, your protection, your
HELPSTATS           T1               avoiding ability, your ability to run away, and your charm.
HELPSTATS           H1CON
HELPSTATS           T1Constitution - Constitution is how well you take a hit.  It determines how
HELPSTATS           T1               well you can take a punch, along with how many hit points
HELPSTATS           T1               you receive each time you go up a level.
HELPSTATS           H                   STATISTIC DESCRIPTION IN SORCERER'S BANE
HELPSTATS           T2The six main statistics, along with random events, determine how well your
HELPSTATS           T1character will perform in Sorcerer's Bane.  The following is a description
HELPSTATS           T1of each one and how each one affects you.  Unlike typical adventure games,
HELPSTATS           T1each statistic will affect the majority of events in Sorcerer's Bane.  (i.e.
HELPSTATS           T1a smart fighter will fight better than a stupid one.)
HELPSTATS           H2DEX
HELPSTATS           T1Dexterity - Dexterity is how agile you are.  It mainly affects your
HELPSTATS           T1            ability to hit an opponent, your protection, your ability
HELPSTATS           T1            to avoid being hit, and your ability to run away.  It also
HELPSTATS           T1            affects your magical resistance, your avoiding ability, your
HELPSTATS           T1            ability to use a magical item, and your alertness.
HELPSTATS           H2CHR
HELPSTATS           T1Charisma - Charisma is how good you look.  It is the major component of
HELPSTATS           T1           your charm, which is used when talking to an opponent or buying
HELPSTATS           T1           from the shop.
HELPSTATS           H2LCK
HELPSTATS           T1LUCK - Luck affects EVERY extended attribute, but only affects them minimally.
HINT1               T1Remember as you adventure to raise your stats some more!
HINT1               T1You'll always do the best you can when they're all at one and a score!
HINT2               T1Dying's only temporary; you save to live again!
HINT2               T1You'll need at least 100 gold and constitution ten!
HINT3               T1Death is not forever! Sometimes life goes on!
HINT3               T1Yet CON may wane, hitpoints drain, and all your gold is gone!
HINT4               T1Pull a Monster Info to see how monster's fight.
HINT4               T1With special powers not seen you may opt for flight.
HINT5               T1Oh to end this dreariness; to sleep; perchance to dream;
HINT5               T1Some monsters are ethereal; some are not what they seem.
HINT6               T1Dragons are big worms, it's true, and no one likes a worm
HINT6               T1But try civility with the GOLD or you may expire your term.
HINT7               T1Not all monsters are so bad; some are really good
HINT7               T1Don't try to tame a bad one though; they act not like a pet should
HINT8               T1Some monsters corrupt, petrify, possess; some even try to drown
HINT8               T1Three is the magic number; don't get caught with your guard down!
HINT9               T1If you want a magic weapon; Bazerker is the best;
HINT9               T1But the price you end up paying may make you want the rest!
HINT10              T1Dispell before you meet big worms or your sure to get a treat!
HINT10              T1Nothing fills the emptyness like a fireball to eat!
HINT11              T1Magic rings are nice to have but not at the shop to buy;
HINT11              T1If you want to leave the shop behind, try the RING OF IDENTIFY
HINT12              T1Books and potions help a lot to bring the stats to rise
HINT12              T1What can help can hinder, though, so identify the prize
HINT13              T1Weapons and armor are removed if there is no curse
HINT13              T1Lerend gets rid of refuse, but don't forget the purse
HINT14              T1You look good; I look good; She looks good they say
HINT14              T1Kiera likes the charm you have and is shows in what you pay
HINT15              T1Life is bitter; life is sweet; can one even tell?
HINT15              T1It's most refreshing at the founts but stats can shrink or swell.
HINT16              T1Have a seat on the throne, old friend, and as we talk in rhyme
HINT16              T1Good or ill may happen there but only at one time.
HINT17              T1Haphestus made a sword for you; a fine sword I would say.
HINT17              T1Bazerker may fight better, but then there's hell to pay!
HINT18              T1You can wield but one thing; not two nor three nor four;
HINT18              T1When instruments fly or weapons go sky, they'll wield themselves some more.
HINT19              T1Be careful of corrosion; it affects all armor and blade
HINT19              T1Unless magic is involved, your items won't be saved!
HINT20              T1Cloaks are worn above your mail, but protection seems so light.
HINT20              T1Yet invisible's more and Elven is four to help you in your fight.
HINT21              T1Treasure sure is nice but monsters are no fun.
HINT21              T1Evading boots pick up the pace when it's time to run.
HINT22              T1Running is a cowards way, and they die a thousand death
HINT22              T1With forward motion, you're here to stay; only once out of breath
HINT23              T1Some monsters seem to move so fast; they're faster than the eye.
HINT23              T1Boots of speed will even things to fight monster on the fly.
HINT24              T1Tiresias was a man who said that he could see
HINT24              T1Put on the ring of blindness and you'll be like Mr. T.
HINT25              T1When speaking of Tiresias, some say that he was blind.
HINT25              T1A ring of sight to see through walls would be a gift most kind.
HINT26              T1Can't seem to get the hitpoints up? Is that why Bunky's down?
HINT26              T1Get a ring of regeneration and turn your life around!
HINT27              T1Rings of regeneration sure do help the healing.
HINT27              T1Other rings disitigrate; It's not the finest feeling.
HINT28              T1Traps are not for the weak of heart; they start to be annoying.
HINT28              T1One of the finest things to wear is a ring of trap avoiding.
HINT29              T1Magic is so hard to get; four new spells every three.
HINT29              T1A ring of enhance magic lets next level's there to see.
HINT30              T1A ring of enhance magic is used by mages the best.
HINT30              T1A fighter gets more tool ability and for spells a little rest.
HINT31              T1A ring of trap generation seems like no attack;
HINT31              T1Nothing bad will happen until you double back.
HINT32              T1Feeling kinda low 'cause you don't have spells to do?
HINT32              T1Wands work well for mages, but for bards and fighters too!
HINT33              T1Read any good books lately? It's nice to read and discover!
HINT33              T1Identify tomes for content first and don't judge them by their cover.
HINT34              T1Some people hate Bazerker; for the price you have to pay
HINT34              T1But with the sorcerer and the Red, there is no better way!
HINT35              T1Collect magic items as you go, and if you have the room,
HINT35              T1Get a ring of dispell magic or prepare to meet thy doom!
HINT36              T1Where are the amulets in this realm? Only one for those with cares.
HINT36              T1Equalizer is its name; it's the one the sorcerer wears.
HINT37              T1Cloaks are worn above your mail, but protection seems so light.
HINT37              T1Yet invisible's more and Elven is four to help you in your fight.
HINT38              T1Red is the weakest worm, yet now spells will not light
HINT38              T1Try regeneration last last last last, and Bazerker in a fight
HINT39              T1Only with the Red worm now should fights sword to claw.
HINT39              T1But fighters need a wand with the others when they want to brawl.
HINT40              T1Resurrection is no fun, but better than being dead.
HINT40              T1That's why Lycantrhopy, possession, and corruption fill one's mind with dread.
HINT41              T1Teleporting works in hard times for those who sell or steal.
HINT41              T1Use magic for the killing blow; it's the last thing that they'll feel.
HINT42              T1A ring of dispell magic is a nice ring to wear around.
HINT42              T1But gas spores blowing up in your face might tend to get you down.
HINT43              T1Luck will help with gambling if you're feeling lucky today.
HINT43              T1There's no luck with the serious one; many things get in the way.
HINT44              T1No need to run from dragons; Use bazerker with the RED.
HINT44              T1Change to Haphestus with the Sorcerer or you'll stay until one's dead!
HINT45              T1Dreams and bears don't hit too much when monsters are immense;
HINT45              T1They can, however, attract the blows when fighting does commence;
HINT46              T1Dispelling magic is a must when large monsters start to balk;
HINT46              T1But what works for Vampires works for Djinnis when you want to wish or talk.
HINT47              T1Talk to the Gambler and see what luck he brings.
HINT47              T1Some really test your trust with a deck of many things.
HINT48              T1A book is ALWAYS nice if there's a wish to gain;
HINT48              T1Gold or Djinnis give 'em too but they can blow away your brain.
HOURSBLOCKED        T A young, scruffy-looking man appears before you.  Although he looks clean,
HOURSBLOCKED        T1his cloths are wrinkled and he appears to have a couple of days growth on
HOURSBLOCKED        T1his face.  He is wearing what appear to be blue jeans, a T-shirt, tennis
HOURSBLOCKED        T1shoes and a baseball cap.  Although definitely not the type to model men's
HOURSBLOCKED        T1underwear in a department store catalogue, he seems likable enough.
HOURSBLOCKED        H2'Hi!'
HOURSBLOCKED        T0, he says casually.
HOURSBLOCKED        H0'My name is Allyn, and I'm the game master around
HOURSBLOCKED        H1here, until they can find somebody to replace me.  (It's a dull job, but
HOURSBLOCKED        H1somebody's gotta do it.)  SB has been blocked out by the SB ADMINISTRATOR
HOURSBLOCKED        H1for a block of hours.  Hey, don't be sad.  Just dial in when it is available.
HOURSBLOCKED        H1We'll be glad to play then.
HOURSBLOCKED        H2Of course, if you had your own copy, you could play when you want.' 
HOURSBLOCKED        T0, he adds
HOURSBLOCKED        T1coyly.
HOURSBLOCKED        T2With that, Allyn disappears. (What a shameless plug, you think.)
INTRODUCTION        H                                INTRODUCTION
INTRODUCTION        W2A young, scruffy-looking man appears before you.  Although he looks clean,
INTRODUCTION        W1his cloths are wrinkled and he appears to have a couple of days growth on
INTRODUCTION        W1his face.  He is wearing what appear to be blue jeans, a T-shirt, tennis
INTRODUCTION        W1shoes and a baseball cap.  Although definitely not the type to model men's
INTRODUCTION        W1underwear in a department store catalogue, he seems likable enough.
INTRODUCTION        T2Hi!
INTRODUCTION        W0, he says casually.
INTRODUCTION        T0My name is Allyn, and I'm the game master around
INTRODUCTION        T1here.  (Actually, I took this job before I knew that there would actually be
INTRODUCTION        T1work involved!)
INTRODUCTION        T2Anyway, welcome to Sorcerer's Bane.  Sorcerer's Bane is a dungeon game of
INTRODUCTION        T1mystery and intrigue where a character which you define runs through the
INTRODUCTION        T1Dungeon of Mogadore to face the evil Lodi himself at the bottom of the
INTRODUCTION        T1dungeon. (Hmmm. I wonder if you're up to it.)
INTRODUCTION        T2The town of Mogadore was supposed to be the site of a magician's academy of
INTRODUCTION        T1mammoth proportions.  Two powerful wizards, Lodi and Sabee, were dedicated to
INTRODUCTION        T1the development of this learning institution.  These wizards developed two
INTRODUCTION        T1STAFFS OF POWER to aid them in both the development of the academy and for
INTRODUCTION        T1protection while they were digging through the dungeons of Mogadore.
INTRODUCTION        H                                INTRODUCTION
INTRODUCTION        T2Ah, but the best made plans often go awry.  For some 'unknown reason', Lodi
INTRODUCTION        T1turned evil.  Lodi killed Sabee by taking him by surprise, in the hopes of
INTRODUCTION        T1combining the near-omnipotent power of the two staffs. (GASP! This is more
INTRODUCTION        T1thrilling than a Dudley Doright rerun.)
INTRODUCTION        T2For some OTHER 'unknown reason', Lodi was not able to manipulate Sabee's staff.
INTRODUCTION        T1Lodi could have destroyed it, I suppose, but with that much magic being
INTRODUCTION        T1released, Lodi's very existence would be threatened.  He opted for the safe
INTRODUCTION        T1route, and divided the staff (carefully!) into four quarterstaffs.  He then
INTRODUCTION        T1tricked, paid, or forced four dragons to guard the four quarterstaffs.
INTRODUCTION        T2Lodi then barred himself at the bottom of his newly-created dungeon to go over
INTRODUCTION        T1plans for the evil domination of the entire realm!  He gathered every type of
INTRODUCTION        T1evil, nasty, and cruel monster to populate the dungeon to stop would-be
INTRODUCTION        T1adventurers (like yourself) from thwarting his plans.
INTRODUCTION        T2Your mission, should you choose to acce . . .(Oops. Wrong script!)  Ahem!  What
INTRODUCTION        T1you need to do is fight your way to the bottom of this dungeon, recover the
INTRODUCTION        T1parts of the other staff, save the girl, (Oops. No girl. Scratch that last one.)
INTRODUCTION        T1answer the 'unknown reasons', and save the realm.  (And YOU thought this would
INTRODUCTION        T1be difficult.)
INTRODUCTION        H                                INTRODUCTION
INTRODUCTION        T2Don't descend into the dungeon too quickly.  The monsters get harder to fight
INTRODUCTION        T1as you descend, and your character must have both the experience and the magic
INTRODUCTION        T1items needed to fight them.
INTRODUCTION        T2You will find magic items and gold in the dungeon left by now-dead adventurers.
INTRODUCTION        T1Magic items need to be either worn or used.  Magic items will not affect your
INTRODUCTION        T1character until they are implemented.  Not all magic items are good, so be
INTRODUCTION        T1careful.
INTRODUCTION        T2Above the dungeon is the town.  You may want to visit some of the stores in
INTRODUCTION        T1town before going too deep into the dungeon.  In town, you can buy magic items,
INTRODUCTION        T1get healed, have cursed items removed, identify items, and much more.  However,
INTRODUCTION        T1all this takes money, so be sure to find some money in the dungeon first.
INTRODUCTION        T2So create a new character and start adventuring.  Be careful, fight smart and
INTRODUCTION        T1well, and you may just survive to tell your tale to others.  Oh yes, and don't
INTRODUCTION        T1forget to print the registration, fill it out, and register.  Good luck, and
INTRODUCTION        T1Godspeed!
MULTIUSERINFO       H                            MULTI-USER SETUP
MULTIUSERINFO       T2Since you have elected the multi-user setup, SB will let you choose several
MULTIUSERINFO       T1options to control the use of SB on your network or PC.  The following fields
MULTIUSERINFO       T1can be entered on the multi-user screen.  If you do not wish to enact any or 
MULTIUSERINFO       T1all of these features, simply leave the field blank.
MULTIUSERINFO       H2MAXIMUM USERS ALLOWED
MULTIUSERINFO       T1This is the number of players allowed to use SB at one time.
MULTIUSERINFO       H2MAX (LIVE) CHARACTERS
MULTIUSERINFO       T1This is the maximum amount of characters any player can
MULTIUSERINFO       T1have running concurrently in SB.  Only live characters count.
MULTIUSERINFO       H                            MULTI-USER SETUP
MULTIUSERINFO       T2Since you have elected the multi-user setup, SB will let you choose several
MULTIUSERINFO       T1options to control the use of SB on your network or PC.  The following fields
MULTIUSERINFO       T1can be entered on the multi-user screen.  If you do not wish to enact any or 
MULTIUSERINFO       T1all of these features, simply leave the field blank.
MULTIUSERINFO       H2STARTING BLOCKED HOUR
MULTIUSERINFO       T1Sometimes a network will be overloaded at specific times during the day
MULTIUSERINFO       T1so that playing a game would be counter-productive.  (Imagine that!)  Hence, 
MULTIUSERINFO       T1SB allows the SBA to declare a 'STARTING BLOCK HOUR' (or SBH).  The SBH is the
MULTIUSERINFO       T1first hour in a block of hours that SB WILL NOT be allowed to be played.  For
MULTIUSERINFO       T1instance, if you wanted to block SB from 8:00 A.M. to 5:00 P.M., your SBH 
MULTIUSERINFO       T1would be 8.  If you don't want to limit when SB can be played at all, simply 
MULTIUSERINFO       T1return through the next question.
MULTIUSERINFO       H2HOURS BLOCKED
MULTIUSERINFO       T1HOURS BLOCKED can disallow access to SB during certain time periods.  This 
MULTIUSERINFO       T1field tells how many hours you want blocked out.
MULTIUSERINFO       H2MAX MINUTES OF PLAY
MULTIUSERINFO       T1This field is used to relegate the amount of contiguous time allowed on playing
MULTIUSERINFO       T1SB.  Again, it is typically for company installations, etc.
MULTIUSERINFO       H                            MULTI-USER SETUP
MULTIUSERINFO       T2Since you have elected the multi-user setup, SB will let you choose several
MULTIUSERINFO       T1options to control the use of SB on your network or PC.  The following fields
MULTIUSERINFO       T1can be entered on the multi-user screen.  If you do not wish to enact any or 
MULTIUSERINFO       T1all of these features, simply leave the field blank.
MULTIUSERINFO       H2SB SYSTEM PASSWORD
MULTIUSERINFO       T1SB allows you, as the system administrator, to configure a system password.
MULTIUSERINFO       T1This would allow you to run ANY PLAYER's characters.  This would be useful
MULTIUSERINFO       T1for answering any questions a user might have OR defining and/or reporting
MULTIUSERINFO       T1any problems that may occur with the SB.
MULTIUSERINFO       T2However, a SB password is a two-edged sword.  If you let your SB password
MULTIUSERINFO       T1get out, it may be possible for any user to reak havok on SB.  This may cause
MULTIUSERINFO       T1more trouble than it's worth.
MULTIUSERINFO       T2Often, a SB ADMINISTRATOR will need to enter into a character's area to resolve
MULTIUSERINFO       T1some conflict or to (hopfully not!!) recreate some bug to be reported.  This
MULTIUSERINFO       T1password allows the SB ADMINISTRATOR to access any user/player running SB.
NOMORECHARS         T2Allyn appears before you.  He has a reproachful look in his eye.
NOMORECHARS         H0  'Now C'MON!'
NOMORECHARS         T0, he says.
NOMORECHARS         H1'You cannot create any more characters!!!  The SB ADMINISTRATOR has, in
NOMORECHARS         H1his or her NEAR INFINITE wisdom, decided to limit the number of characters any
NOMORECHARS         H1one user can play.  This is to save both system resources AND to make SB a more
NOMORECHARS         H1manageable game.  Even I can't go against the SBA (and I usually run things
NOMORECHARS         H1around here).  Play with the character(s) you've got (until they win or die)
NOMORECHARS         H1and then you can create more.  (That's all we need around here is more
NOMORECHARS         H1unbridled enthusiasm.  WHEN are these ROOKIES going to leave me alone!!!)
NOMORECHARS         T2With that, he disappears.  As you leave, you start to mumble about letting your
NOMORECHARS         H1job affect your personal life.
NOMOREUSERS         T A young, scruffy-looking man appears before you.  Although he looks clean,
NOMOREUSERS         T1his cloths are wrinkled and he appears to have a couple of days growth on
NOMOREUSERS         T1his face.  He is wearing what appear to be blue jeans, a T-shirt, tennis
NOMOREUSERS         T1shoes and a baseball cap.  Although definitely not the type to model men's
NOMOREUSERS         T1underwear in a department store catalogue, he seems likable enough.
NOMOREUSERS         H2'Hi!'
NOMOREUSERS         T0, he says casually.
NOMOREUSERS         H0'My name is Allyn, and I'm the game master around
NOMOREUSERS         H1here, until they can find somebody to replace me.  (It's a dull job, but
NOMOREUSERS         H1somebody's gotta do it.)  Now, I know you didn't pull this game up to hear any
NOMOREUSERS         H1"No room at the inn" story, but as it stands, SB cannot handle any more users
NOMOREUSERS         H1Without the help of the SB ADMINISTRATOR.
NOMOREUSERS         H2You can either have the SB ADMINISTRATOR reconfigure the system OR buy your
NOMOREUSERS         H1own copy.'
NOMOREUSERS         T2With that, Allyn disappears. (What a shameless plug, you think.)
RESURRECTION        T You seem to float above your body, helplessly drifting to meet your fate.  All
RESURRECTION        T1of life's little mysteries are about to be answered (just what you wanted, eh?)
RESURRECTION        T1when all of a sudden, a bright flash encompasses the area.  Allyn, Lerend, and
RESURRECTION        T1Kiera appear.
RESURRECTION        H2'Well,'
RESURRECTION        T0, Allyn says,
RESURRECTION        H0 'we need this guy pretty bad.  It's our only hope against
RESURRECTION        H1Lodi.'
RESURRECTION        G1'Our ONLY hope?'
RESURRECTION        T0 asks Kiera.
RESURRECTION        G0 'Gee, things are looking pretty bleak, aren't
RESURRECTION        G1they?'
RESURRECTION        W1'Indeed.'
RESURRECTION        T0 adds Lerend.
RESURRECTION        W0 'Death tends to hamper adventuring in most cases.'
RESURRECTION        T1Allyn looks irritated.
RESURRECTION        H0 'Look, I'm irritated.  If this guy continues to be dead,
RESURRECTION        H1the worst will happen!'
RESURRECTION        T1Kiera looks thoughtful.
RESURRECTION        G0 'Would the worst be that Lodi would usurp your job as a
RESURRECTION        G1reasonably benevolent game master?'
RESURRECTION        H1'Nope.  I already offered.  He's not interested.'
RESURRECTION        W1'There is no excuse for sloth'
RESURRECTION        T0, Lerend says gravely.
RESURRECTION        H1'The WORST would be that we have to bring down another GREEN, INEXPERIENCED,
RESURRECTION        H1LOUSY, GOOD-FOR-NOTHING LOSER like this dead guy.'
RESURRECTION        T0  You feel indignant.
RESURRECTION        H0 'Just
RESURRECTION        H1see what you can do.'
RESURRECTION        T2With that, Allyn disappears. (Near-omnipotent beings tend to do that.)
RESURRECTION        T As soon as Allyn leaves, Lerend immediately removes your gold pouch.  Staring
RESURRECTION        T1down Kiera's icy glare, Lerend feebly responds,
RESURRECTION        W0 'Any metal will hinder
RESURRECTION        W1the healing process.'
RESURRECTION        T0  Kiera just shakes her head as Lerend puts your pouch
RESURRECTION        T1away "for safe keeping."
RESURRNOGOLD        T2After examining the contents of the pouch, Lerend begins to look EXTREMELY
RESURRNOGOLD        T1distracted.  Although he tries to mumble some feeble prayer, you start to feel
RESURRNOGOLD        T1yourself slipping away.  Your last words you hear are Kiera mumbling to Lerend:
RESURRNOGOLD        G2'S'allright, Ler.  Ya cure some, ya lose some.'
RESURRNOCON         T2Lerend places his hands on you and starts to pray.  This seems to go on for
RESURRNOCON         T1hours until, finally, Lerend starts to sag, sway, sache, and swoon.  You feel
RESURRNOCON         T1yourself slipping away as the prayer fails and Lerend hits the floor.  The last
RESURRNOCON         T1thing you hear is Kiera sobbing.
RESURRNOCON         T2She's probably upset that she didn't think of grabbing your gold pouch first.
RESURRWORKED        T2Lerend places his hand on you and starts to pray.  After hours and hours of
RESURRWORKED        T1prayer, you feel yourself being tugged back into your body.  Lerend finally
RESURRWORKED        T1collapses from the strain.
RESURRWORKED        T2After a minute or two, Kiera gasps as you start to get up.  She then smiles,
RESURRWORKED        T1gives your her business card and teleports out. Two acolytes teleport in and
RESURRWORKED        T1start to give Lerend CPR.  As Lerend starts to recover, you begin to realize
RESURRWORKED        T1that you feel pretty bad.  Dying sure does take a toll on a person.
SETUP               H                      SORCERER'S BANE (v1.1) -- SYSTEM SETUP
SETUP               T2Welcome to Sorcerer's Bane.  It appears you are the first one to run SB on
SETUP               T1this system, so I will have to ask you a few questions about the way you want
SETUP               T1SB administered.
SETUP               H                      SORCERER'S BANE (v1.1) -- SYSTEM SETUP
SETUP               T2Since you are the first one to run SB on this system, you will have to decide
SETUP               T1how you want SB administered.
SETUP               T2You have your choice of whether to set up SB as multi-user or single-user.
SETUP               T1The following is a guide as to which setup is best for you.
SETUP               T2A single-user setup is when you are not running over a network and you either
SETUP               T1are the only one who accesses this game OR you trust the other computer users
SETUP               T1enough to not worry about password protection.  I would imagine that most of
SETUP               T1you would pick single-user so as to not mess with passwords and the like.
SETUP               T2A multi-user setup would configure the game to accept several users at one 
SETUP               T1time.  This configuration would also be chosen if you several people had 
SETUP               T1access to this computer AND you did not want them using your characters.  In 
SETUP               T1the multi-user setup, password protection is enforced.
SETUPADMIN          H                      SORCERER'S BANE (v1.1) -- SYSTEM SETUP
SETUPADMIN          T2With great power comes great responsibility.  Because you are the first person
SETUPADMIN          T1to use this system, you are now the defacto SB ADMINISTRATOR.  This will give
SETUPADMIN          T1you powers and abilities beyond those of mortal players.
SETUPADMIN          T2Remember, this is your system, so answer the following questions carefully.  If
SETUPADMIN          T1you aren't careful, SB may take up a large chunk of your resources or too much
SETUPADMIN          T1of your prime system hours.  Fortunately for you, SB has been created to allow
SETUPADMIN          T1the SB ADMINISTARTOR to limit SB usage on multi-user systems.  On the following
SETUPADMIN          T1screen, you will be asked questions about configuring the SB system.
SETUPADMIN          H                      SORCERER'S BANE (v1.1) -- SYSTEM SETUP
SETUPADMIN          T2SB allows you, as the system administrator, to configure a system password.
SETUPADMIN          T1This would allow you to run ANY PLAYER's characters.  This would be useful
SETUPADMIN          T1for answering any questions a user might have OR defining and/or reporting
SETUPADMIN          T1any problems that may occur with the SB.
SETUPADMIN          T2However, a SB password is a two-edged sword.  If you let your SB password
SETUPADMIN          T1get out, it may be possible for any user to reak havok on SB.  This may cause
SETUPADMIN          T1more trouble than it's worth.
SETUPBLOCK          T1Sometimes a network will be overloaded at specific times during the day
SETUPBLOCK          T1so that playing a game would be counter-productive.  (Imagine that!)  Hence, SB
SETUPBLOCK          T1allows the SBA to declare a 'STARTING BLOCK HOUR' (or SBH).  The SBH is the
SETUPBLOCK          T1first hour in a block of hours that SB WILL NOT be allowed to be played.  For
SETUPBLOCK          T1instance, if you wanted to block SB from 8:00 A.M. to 5:00 P.M., your SBH would
SETUPBLOCK          T1be 8.  If you don't want to limit when SB can be played at all, simply return
SETUPBLOCK          T1through the next question.
SETUPSAVE           T2If you want to save these options, answer YES.  If you decide not to save
SETUPSAVE           T1these options, answer NO.  If you answer YES, the only way to change your answer
SETUPSAVE           T1is to reinstall SB.  If you answer NO, nothing will be saved and you will be
SETUPSAVE           T1returned to the system.
SETUPSCOPE          T The following questions allow you to limit the scope of your system.  If you
SETUPSCOPE          T1want unlimited scope to any of the questions below, leave that question blank.
SETUPSCOPE          T1(i.e. If you want each player to be able to play as long as he/she wants to,
SETUPSCOPE          T1simply return through the 'maximum number of minutes' question.)
SORCERERSLAIR       H                        THE LAIR OF LODI THE SORCERER
SORCERERSLAIR       T2You have found what may be the culmination of your quest!  You have found the
SORCERERSLAIR       T1sorcerer's lair!  Around you are potions and every sort of magical artifact.
SORCERERSLAIR       T1The air smells of chemicals and static electricity.
SORCERERSLAIR       T2Lodi himself is sitting on a throne at the other end of the room!  The years
SORCERERSLAIR       T1sure have been rough on him.  Magic looks like it has taken it's toll!
SORCERERSLAIR       R2'Good day traveler.'
SORCERERSLAIR       T0, He rasply intones.
SORCERERSLAIR       R0 'It's as good a day as any to die!'
SORCERERSLAIR       T2(You guess talking him out of being evil just isn't going to work.)
SORCERERSLAIR       M2'I'm ready when you are.'  
SORCERERSLAIR       T0 You confidently intone.  
SORCERERSLAIR       M0 'One way or another, Lodi,
SORCERERSLAIR       M1 you're going down today!'
SORCERERSLAIR       T2You laugh to yourself.  Confidence is easy when you hold Gilcrest, the good
SORCERERSLAIR       T1staff of power.  You draw Gilcrest out while Lodi draws out Moshannon!  You
SORCERERSLAIR       T1gulp as Moshannon looks just like Gilcrest!  This may indeed be a long fight!
SORCERERSLAIR       H                        THE LAIR OF LODI THE SORCERER
SORCERERSLAIR       T2Just then, to your and Lodi's amazement, the staffs start conversing!
SORCERERSLAIR       B2'Now is the time, Gilcrest.'
SORCERERSLAIR       T0 Moshannon speaks with no emotion.
SORCERERSLAIR       T2(Great.  Another dry monotone boring staff.  You fight the urge to drift off.)
SORCERERSLAIR       H2'Aye, Moshannon.  Do you wish to involve these others?'
SORCERERSLAIR       B2'I see no need for that.  Eventually, it always seems to come down to good
SORCERERSLAIR       B1versus evil.'
SORCERERSLAIR       T2With that, both staffs fly out and collide.  They both seemed to vanish upon
SORCERERSLAIR       T1collission, obviously intent on taking their dispute elsewhere.
SORCERERSLAIR       T2For a brief moment, Lodi and you forget your differences.  Lodi looks stunned.
SORCERERSLAIR       R2'I didn't know they could talk!' 
SORCERERSLAIR       T0  He shakenly says.
SORCERERSLAIR       M2'Maybe it just never had anything to say.'
SORCERERSLAIR       T0 you chuckle.
SORCERERSLAIR       T2Lodi is then snapped out of his musing.
SORCERERSLAIR       R0 'This is all YOUR fault!'
SORCERERSLAIR       T0 he screeches.
SORCERERSLAIR       H                        THE LAIR OF LODI THE SORCERER
SORCERERSLAIR       T2NUTS! You were hoping that losing Moshannon would revert Lodi back to a
SORCERERSLAIR       T1reasonably benevolent entity.  Evidently absolute power does indeed corrupt
SORCERERSLAIR       T1absolutely.  A fight may be inevitable.
SORCERERSLAIR       M2'It seems to me that there is no longer a need for fighting.'
SORCERERSLAIR       T0 You calmly intone.
SORCERERSLAIR       R2'Coward!! Today, you die!!!'
SORCERERSLAIR       T2It seems that fighting is going to be the only way to resolve this meeting.  You
SORCERERSLAIR       T1both square off for what is quite possibly the fight of your life.
STAFFOFPOWER        T Ah, finally you have all of the components for the staff of power!  As you
STAFFOFPOWER        T1finish assembling it, it starts to speak! (Well, not actually speak.  It seems
STAFFOFPOWER        T1to just think what it wants to say, and those thoughts appear in your brain.
STAFFOFPOWER        T1Well, not actually appear, but. . . Well, you know what I mean.)
STAFFOFPOWER        M2'Hello, my name is Gilcrest, and I am one of two STAFFS OF POWER in existence.'
STAFFOFPOWER        T2Hmmm. Dry and boring, but in a monotone.  Oh well.  Whaddaya want from a piece
STAFFOFPOWER        T1of wood?  The staff continues:
STAFFOFPOWER        M2'The other STAFF OF POWER is named "Moshannon".  We were first created by the
STAFFOFPOWER        M1combined magical talents of both Lodi and Sabee.  So much magic was embued
STAFFOFPOWER        M1that we are now the most powerful artifacts in the realms.
STAFFOFPOWER        M2'So much power was instilled in Moshannon and I that we started polarizing
STAFFOFPOWER        M1the very forces of good and evil.  Good polarized around myself, while evil
STAFFOFPOWER        M1polarized around Moshannon, thereby making us even more powerful, as well as
STAFFOFPOWER        M1sentient.
STAFFOFPOWER        T2(Gasp!  The raw power this artifact must exude must be off the scale!)
STAFFOFPOWER        M 'Lodi was intially alligned with the forces of good, but continued use of the
STAFFOFPOWER        M1evil staff, Moshannon, caused him to slowly revert to evil.  After becoming
STAFFOFPOWER        M1totally corrupt, he killed Sabee.  He then tried to wield both myself and
STAFFOFPOWER        M1Moshannon, thereby doubling his power.  I then tried to convert him back to
STAFFOFPOWER        M1being the good wizard he was, but Moshannon prevented this and coerced Lodi
STAFFOFPOWER        M1into dividing me into four staffs, thereby reducing my power.
STAFFOFPOWER        M2'You must go down one level to where Lodi is.  I will take care of Moshannon.
STAFFOFPOWER        M1After that, I will no longer be able to help you.  You must deal with the
STAFFOFPOWER        M1evil wizard, Lodi, yourself.'
STAFFOFPOWER        T2Oh is that all!  Only the realm's most powerful magician, and Gilcrest wants
STAFFOFPOWER        T1you to take him on alone.  You vow to take a desk job when and if you return
STAFFOFPOWER        T1from this misadventure.
TIMESUP             H1'Sorry, chum, but play time's over.  The SB administrator has (probably wisely)
TIMESUP             H1limited the time allowed for the game to be played and YOU (God love ya!) have
TIMESUP             H1maxed it out.  Probably it's time to get back to work and get on with your life.
TIMESUP             H2However, if you have no life and your work is meaningless, or time constraints
TIMESUP             H1serve only to annoy you, you could take desitiny into you own hands and buy your
TIMESUP             H1own copy!'
TIMESUP             T2With that, Allyn disappears. (What a shameless plug, you think.)
WEARWIELD           T Kiera radiantly beams as she quickly takes your gold. (You wonder if you could
WEARWIELD           T1have gotten it cheaper, but then dismiss the thought.)  'You know,' Kieara
WEARWIELD           T1says, 'you're all right.  I'm sure you'll be happy with your new item.
WEARWIELD           T2'You seem like a pretty shrewd adventurer.  There are some who come in here
WEARWIELD           T1that purchase items but then don't use them, as if carrying the item were
WEARWIELD           T1enough.' She chuckles.
WEARWIELD           T2'Remember, you have to WEAR (or WIELD) a weapon, armor, ring, etc., and you
WEARWIELD           T1have to USE potions, wands, and books to implement them.  To do this, you have
WEARWIELD           T1to try INVENTORY MANAGEMENT.  You seem smart enough to know this already.'
WEARWIELD           T2Of course you know this already.  Doesn't everyone?
WINGAME             H                      YOU HAVE BECOME 'THE SORCERER'S BANE'
WINGAME             T2Lodi looks at you with a surprised look.  It seems hard for him to believe that
WINGAME             T1his end is finally here.
WINGAME             T2As Lodi breaths his last, you can't help but feel sorry for the guy.  He was
WINGAME             T1truly a victim of fate; first being corrupted then hunted and finally killed.
WINGAME             T1Sometimes the senselessness of evil infuriates you.  Perhaps if things could
WINGAME             T1have been different, Lodi would have been a benefit to the realms.
WINGAME             T2You are snapped out of your reverie as the dungeon around you starts to
WINGAME             T1collapse!  Lodi's magic must have held the structure together.  With no
WINGAME             T1where to run, you feel certain death must follow.
WINGAME             T2All at once, in the midst of the falling rubble, Kiera teleports in.
WINGAME             G2'Grab my hand NOW!'
WINGAME             T0 she screams.  As you comply, she telports you and herself
WINGAME             T1out of the collapsing dungeon!
WINGAME             H                      YOU HAVE BECOME 'THE SORCERER'S BANE'
WINGAME             T2You thank Kiera for saving your life.  She teleports out as Allyn teleports in.
WINGAME             H2'Well, after all of those other adventurers failed, you succeeded.  It's really
WINGAME             H1a shame.  Lodi used to be a real asset to the realm.'
WINGAME             M2'There still remains one question to be answered.'
WINGAME             T0, you say.
WINGAME             M0  'I am the
WINGAME             M1Sorcerer's Bane, but you seem much more powerful than I am.  Why couldn't
WINGAME             M1you have taken Lodi out yourself?  You know, "cut out the middleman"?'
WINGAME             T2Allyn looks away with a rare sad look in his eye.
WINGAME             H2'I could have perhaps stopped Lodi and Sabee, but as game master, I must expend
WINGAME             H1most of my energy on maintaining the balance of good and evil in the realms.
WINGAME             H1An imbalance would certainly destroy us all except the most pious or most evil.
WINGAME             H2'The last game master went hopelessly insane. Having all this power, but no way
WINGAME             H1to use it is certainly maddening at times.
WINGAME             T2With that, Allyn teleports out.  It's seems strange to you that the most
WINGAME             T1powerful entity in the realm is probably more a prisoner of his own
WINGAME             T1responsibility than anything else in the realms.
WINGAME             H                      YOU HAVE BECOME 'THE SORCERER'S BANE'
WINGAME             T2At last your mission is done.  You are the true hero of the realms.
WINGAME             T1You head out for other adventures.  Perhaps more will be coming your way.
WINGAME             T2Don't forget to register, or at least tell all you friends.  And, if enough
WINGAME             T1people register, look soon for 'The return of the Sorcerer's Bane:  The 
WINGAME             T1search for Allyn.'  With more laughs (of the BWAA-HA-HA-HA variety), more
WINGAME             T1graphics (GASP!  Real Picutures!) and more mindless waste of your computer time.
WINGAME             T2(Of course, if not enough people register, look soon for me at the unemployment
WINGAME             T1line.  BWAA-HA-HA-HA!!)
